{
  "version": "1.0",
  "name": "Quantum 3: Orchestrated Objective Reduction",
  "description": "Orch-OR: A Quantum Theory of Consciousness\n\nTHE BIG IDEA (Penrose-Hameroff):\nConsciousness is not computation - it's something that happens when \nquantum superpositions in brain microtubules undergo \"objective reduction\" \n(collapse) due to quantum gravity effects.\n\nWHY \"ORCHESTRATED\"?\nThe collapses are not random - they're orchestrated by:\n  1. Synaptic inputs that tune microtubule quantum states\n  2. Dendritic-somatic integration\n  3. Gap junction connections between neurons\n  4. Coherent 40 Hz gamma oscillations\n\nTHE MODEL (this network):\n\n[Sensory] --> [Microtubule 1] --                                 [Memory] ---> [Microtubule 2] ----> [Orchestration] --> [Collapse] --> [Conscious Moment]\n                                 /\n[Emotion] --> [Microtubule 3] --/\n\nThree quantum microtubules represent different brain regions processing \ndifferent information streams. They are \"orchestrated\" together, and \nwhen collapse occurs, it produces a unified conscious moment.\n\nKEY PARAMETERS:\n- collapse_threshold: How much coherence before collapse (lower = more frequent conscious moments)\n- coherence_time: How long quantum effects persist (longer = more quantum computation before collapse)\n- entanglement_range: How far quantum correlations spread\n\nPENROSE'S CLAIM:\nThis collapse is non-computable - it cannot be simulated by a classical \ncomputer (like this one!). So this tutorial is a rough approximation \nof what might really be quantum gravity effects at the Planck scale.\n\nThe debate continues: Is consciousness computable? Can AI ever be conscious?\nOr does true consciousness require quantum gravity?",
